WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
We are all done with our project, but before we move on I want to do a quick recap on the

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.039
three server primitives that we learned about, so tools, resources, and prompts. In particular,

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:13.680
I want to highlight something interesting about each of these, namely what part of an app is

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.559
really responsible for running each. In other words, in a typical application, who is really

5
00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:23.520
running each of these things and who benefits from them? Well, we would say that tools are

6
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.799
model-controlled. This means that Cloud alone is really responsible for deciding when to run

7
00:00:28.799 --> 00:00:35.200
a given tool. Resources are app-controlled. In other words, some code running inside of your app

8
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.680
is going to decide that it needs some data provided by a resource. It will be your app's

9
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.360
code that decides to execute a resource and use the returned data in some way, maybe by using that

10
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.959
data in the UI or something like that. In our case, we fetch a resource and then use that data

11
00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:57.119
inside the UI to provide a list of autocomplete options. We also fetch a resource to augment a

12
00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:02.240
prompt. Both those things were really application-related code that was authored by you and

13
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.199
I to put together. And finally, prompts are really user-controlled. So a user decides when a prompt

14
00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:14.639
is going to run. A user might start the invocation of a prompt by clicking on some UI element,

15
00:01:14.639 --> 00:01:19.839
like a button or a menu option, or they might make use of a slash command, which is what we did.

16
00:01:19.839 --> 00:01:25.040
The reason I highlight what is controlling each of these is to give you some idea of their purpose.

17
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.000
So if you ever need to add capabilities to Cloud, you're probably going to want to look

18
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
at implementing some tools inside of your MCP server or consuming some server's tools through

19
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.599
your MCP client. If you ever want to get some data into your app for the purposes of showing content

20
00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:47.040
in the UI or something similar, then you probably want to use a resource. And if you ever want to

21
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.559
implement some kind of predefined workflow, you probably want to look at prompts. Now you can see

22
00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:59.680
examples of all these ideas inside of the official Cloud interface at cloud.ai. So here's what it

23
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.480
currently looks like for me. You'll notice that underneath the main chat input are some buttons

24
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.720
right here. If I click on one and then click on one of these examples, you'll see that I immediately

25
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:17.119
dive into a chat. So this was a user-controlled action. I, as the user, decided to start up this

26
00:02:17.119 --> 00:02:21.520
particular workflow, and I'm making use of a prompt that was probably already written ahead

27
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.320
of time and probably has been optimized in some way. So to implement that list of buttons right

28
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.720
there, we would probably want to put together a series of different prompts inside of a MCP server.

29
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.559
Likewise, if I go back and maybe click on this little tab right here, the plus button, you'll

30
00:02:36.559 --> 00:02:40.800
notice that I have a add from Google Drive button. Now, I'm not going to click on it because it's

31
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.639
going to show some of my internal documents, but if I click on that button, I'm going to see some

32
00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:50.080
documents that I can add into this chat as some context. Knowing what documents to actually

33
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.880
render in that list, and then whenever I click on one, automatically injecting its contents into

34
00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.479
the context of this chat, that is all application-related code. So it is solely the application

35
00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:05.839
that needs to know the list of documents to render here. And that's, again, specifically UI-related

36
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:11.039
elements. So to implement that listing of documents from Google Drive, I would probably look at

37
00:03:11.039 --> 00:03:16.880
implementing a resource inside of a MCP server. And then finally, if I enter in a message to this

38
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.360
chat of something like, what is square root of three, use JavaScript to calculate the value and

39
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.800
send it off, I'm clearly expecting Claude to somehow execute some JavaScript code, which would

40
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.559
likely be done through the use of a tool. In this case, the decision to use a tool was 100%

41
00:03:32.559 --> 00:03:38.000
model-controlled. It is the model that decided to use some JavaScript tool execution. To implement

42
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.759
something like this inside of a MCP server, we'd likely want to, you guessed it, provide a tool.

43
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.919
So in total, that's our three different server primitives. And each one is really intended to be

44
00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:54.559
used by a different portion of your overall application. So we've got tools, which are

45
00:03:54.559 --> 00:03:59.119
generally going to serve your model, resources, which are generally going to serve your app,

46
00:03:59.119 --> 00:04:04.479
and prompts, which are going to serve your users. And once again, these are high-level guidelines.

47
00:04:04.479 --> 00:04:08.320
And the only reason I mention them is to just give you a sense of when you should use each of

48
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.479
these primitives, depending upon what you are trying to put together.